WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:04.292
For this project, I used ChatGPT to assist with research, coding, financial

00:00:04.292 --> 00:00:05.720
calculations, and validation.

00:00:06.160 --> 00:00:08.560
The analysis and investment decisions are my own.

00:00:08.800 --> 00:00:12.000
This project is for educational purposes and is not investment advice.

00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:16.812
The two main Python files are engine.py which performs the financial

00:00:16.812 --> 00:00:20.640
calculations, and app.py which controls the user interface.

00:00:23.320 --> 00:00:29.080
The application uses 60 monthly total returns for six ETFs from November

00:00:29.080 --> 00:00:35.160
2021 through October 2026 to estimate returns, volatility, and relationships

00:00:35.160 --> 00:00:36.360
between assets.

00:00:36.360 --> 00:00:42.208
Starting with engine.py, the estimate_from function calculates annual return

00:00:42.208 --> 00:00:46.440
estimates by multiplying average monthly returns by 12.

00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:51.113
It also annualizes the covariance matrix, which accounts for how assets move

00:00:51.113 --> 00:00:51.600
together.

00:00:52.640 --> 00:00:57.074
The metrics function calculates gross return, net return after trading

00:00:57.074 --> 00:01:01.699
costs, portfolio volatility, and turnover, which measures how much of the

00:01:01.699 --> 00:01:03.599
portfolio must be reallocated.

00:01:04.960 --> 00:01:10.373
The optimize function evaluates 53,130 possible portfolios using allocation

00:01:10.373 --> 00:01:11.600
increments of 5%.

00:01:11.920 --> 00:01:17.743
It filters these portfolios based on my 6% minimum estimated net return, 15%

00:01:17.743 --> 00:01:23.030
maximum volatility, 5% minimum BIL allocation, and 25% maximum annual

00:01:23.030 --> 00:01:23.720
turnover.

00:01:24.440 --> 00:01:27.920
Of these portfolios, 1,082 meet all four requirements.

00:01:28.480 --> 00:01:32.916
The function selects the portfolio with the lowest volatility and the

00:01:32.916 --> 00:01:37.544
portfolio with the highest estimated Sharpe ratio, which measures excess

00:01:37.544 --> 00:01:41.080
return relative to risk using a Treasury rate of 4.37%.

00:01:43.960 --> 00:01:46.040
Moving on to app.py, the file.

00:01:46.040 --> 00:01:50.040
This file allows users to adjust investment requirements and assumptions.

00:01:50.080 --> 00:01:54.987
These inputs are passed into the review function from engine.py, which

00:01:54.987 --> 00:01:57.440
recalculates the portfolio results.

00:02:00.920 --> 00:02:05.524
The application displays the feasible portfolios, efficient frontier and

00:02:05.524 --> 00:02:10.320
portfolio allocations, allowing users to compare estimated return and risk.

00:02:12.920 --> 00:02:16.520
Moving on to the test_engine.py file.

00:02:16.720 --> 00:02:21.356
This file contains 22 automated tests covering financial calculations, data

00:02:21.356 --> 00:02:25.560
integrity, portfolio selection, constraints, and failure conditions.

00:02:25.880 --> 00:02:27.200
All 22 tests pass.

00:02:27.200 --> 00:02:31.216
My dad also successfully launched the application on a second computer using

00:02:31.216 --> 00:02:34.280
the README in approximately 8 to 9 minutes without errors.

00:02:36.720 --> 00:02:41.134
For my changed-input experiment, I wrote and committed my prediction to

00:02:41.134 --> 00:02:45.485
GitHub before reducing SOXX's estimated annual return by 10 percentage

00:02:45.485 --> 00:02:45.920
points.

00:02:45.960 --> 00:02:52.781
I expected SOXX's allocation to decrease along with the estimated return and

00:02:52.781 --> 00:02:56.640
volatility of the maximum-Sharpe portfolio.

00:03:00.480 --> 00:03:07.568
And as you can see, as I reduce SOXX's estimated annual return by 10 points,

00:03:07.568 --> 00:03:14.563
by 10 percentage points, the minimum-volatility portfolio remains unchanged

00:03:14.563 --> 00:03:18.480
because it already had no SOXX allocation.

00:03:18.480 --> 00:03:24.632
The maximum-Sharpe portfolio reduces SOXX from 15% to 5%, reallocating those

00:03:24.632 --> 00:03:28.680
10 percentage points equally between QQQ and SCHD.

00:03:29.280 --> 00:03:35.647
Its estimated net return decreases from 12.24% to 10.03%, while volatility

00:03:35.647 --> 00:03:38.400
decreases from 14.18% to 12.44%.

00:03:38.920 --> 00:03:44.364
The Sharpe ratio also decreases from 0.555 to 0.455, more than I originally

00:03:44.364 --> 00:03:49.880
expected because excess return declined proportionally more than volatility.

00:03:51.400 --> 00:03:55.816
Portfolios still met my requirements, so my paper trading recommendation

00:03:55.816 --> 00:03:56.920
remains unchanged.

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:06.832
Finally, if I enter a current account drawdown of 15%, the application stops

00:04:06.832 --> 00:04:13.574
recommending new risk-taking, demonstrating that risk controls can override

00:04:13.574 --> 00:04:17.080
otherwise acceptable portfolio results.

00:04:17.720 --> 00:04:21.493
The biggest limitation is that estimated returns are based on historical

00:04:21.493 --> 00:04:25.319
performance, which is why I recommend paper trading before committing any

00:04:25.319 --> 00:04:26.000
real capital.

